The fire in the middle of the hall, banked and tended, with a
pot on it that has been simmering longer than anyone here has
been alive.

Nobody built this house. It is dressed stone, in a clearing, in
an oak wood, on an island that sits off no coast at all. It is
spotless. There are no servants.

Four women work in it, or what look like women. They are
daughters of the springs, the groves, the rivers that run down
to the sea. They set the table. They mix the wine. They do not
speak to anybody, including her.
